We can rewrite the denominator as $n(n+1)$.
Then, we can rewrite the fraction as $\frac{1}{n}-\frac{1}{n+1}$.
The sum telescopes, meaning many of the terms will cancel out:
\begin{align*}
\sum_{n=1}^{1000} \frac{1}{n^2 + n} &= \sum_{n=1}^{1000} \left(\frac{1}{n}-\frac{1}{n+1}\right) \\
&= \left(\frac{1}{1}-\frac{1}{2}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{3}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{3}-\frac{1}{4}\right) + \dots + \left(\frac{1}{1000}-\frac{1}{1001}\right) \\
&= 1-\frac{1}{1001} \\
&= \boxed{\frac{1000}{1001}}.
\end{align*}
The answer is: \frac{1000}{1001}

