To find the decimal representation of $\frac{1}{13}$, we perform long division:
$$
\begin{array}{c|ccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0.} & 0 & 0 \\
\cline{2-4}
13 & 1 & . & 0 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{1} & 3 \\
\cline{2-3}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & 7 & 0 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 6 & 5 \\
\cline{3-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 3 & 5 & 0 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 3 & 2 & 5 \\
\cline{4-5}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 2 & 5 & 0 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 2 & 3 & 6 \\
\cline{4-6}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & 1 & 4 & 0 \\
\end{array}
$$
We see that the decimal representation of $\frac{1}{13}$ is $0.\overline{076923}$, which means that the digits after the decimal point repeat in the pattern $076923$.

To find the 43rd digit, we need to find the remainder when 43 is divided by 6 (the length of the repeating pattern). Since $43 = 6 \cdot 7 + 1$, the 43rd digit is the same as the 1st digit, which is $\boxed{0}$.

The answer is: 0

